1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,880
Over ten years, stocks outgrew homes
almost nine times in ten.

2
00:00:05,867 --> 00:00:10,747
So renting looks smart. Nina rents, with
$92,000 saved.

3
00:00:10,934 --> 00:00:16,667
If her renting twin held cash, her buying
twin ended ahead about 3 in 4 times.

4
00:00:16,827 --> 00:00:21,707
With that cash in stocks instead, the
buyer led only about half the time.

5
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:27,900
Over fifteen years, cash kept the buyer
ahead every time;

6
00:00:27,980 --> 00:00:30,460
stocks, only about 1 in 5.

7
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:34,220
So if Nina rents and invests the
difference,

8
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:36,220
does she end up ahead of owning?

9
00:00:36,403 --> 00:00:40,620
Stocks outgrew the house almost nine times
out of ten,

10
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:43,820
yet buying still came out ahead in half.

11
00:00:44,033 --> 00:00:46,540
It's history, not a forecast.

12
00:00:46,913 --> 00:00:53,820
We replayed every start month since 1991
as a house on one long street:

13
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:57,500
lit if the buyer ends ahead, dark if not.

14
00:00:57,720 --> 00:01:04,060
The renting twin's pile is the $92,000,
plus whatever owning would have cost each

15
00:01:04,127 --> 00:01:05,740
month beyond her rent.

16
00:01:05,980 --> 00:01:09,260
All of it goes into the whole US stock
market,

17
00:01:09,313 --> 00:01:11,500
every month, through every drop.

18
00:01:15,333 --> 00:01:19,653
The first house on that street is January
1991.

19
00:01:19,877 --> 00:01:26,877
With the renting twin's pile kept in cash,
the buyer finished ten years $48,410

20
00:01:28,466 --> 00:01:29,466
ahead.

21
00:01:30,043 --> 00:01:37,043
With the pile in stocks, the same buyer
finished $485,063 behind.

22
00:01:38,747 --> 00:01:45,227
Same house, same mortgage, same rent; the
only thing that moved was the renter's

23
00:01:45,323 --> 00:01:46,747
pile.

24
00:01:47,733 --> 00:01:53,093
In those ten years, a dollar in the whole
US stock market grew almost five times.

25
00:01:53,113 --> 00:01:56,613
And the renter's pile was never a single
dollar:

26
00:01:56,666 --> 00:02:02,053
it was the $92,000 on day one, plus a
deposit every month.

27
00:02:02,169 --> 00:02:05,493
Whenever owning cost more than renting
that month,

28
00:02:05,513 --> 00:02:09,173
the gap went into her pile, and the market
grew that too.

29
00:02:09,546 --> 00:02:16,373
So one change, stocks instead of cash,
turned the first house from lit to dark.

30
00:02:17,367 --> 00:02:21,367
A lot of rent-versus-buy math assumes one
growth rate;

31
00:02:21,431 --> 00:02:25,207
this replays what actually happened, month
by month.

32
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:30,540
The renting twin's rent is an assumption,
the tag on her door:

33
00:02:30,572 --> 00:02:35,020
5 percent of the price a year, as of July
2026,

34
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:37,180
and later, that tag decides.

35
00:02:37,607 --> 00:02:41,900
So, with the pile in stocks, how much of
the street stays lit?

36
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:47,700
After ten years, a house is lit if the
buying twin's equity,

37
00:02:47,753 --> 00:02:51,620
after selling costs, is bigger than the
renting twin's pile.

38
00:02:51,860 --> 00:02:55,060
If the pile is bigger, the door goes dark.

39
00:02:55,348 --> 00:03:02,348
That's 307 houses, from January 1991 to
the last start with ten full years of

40
00:03:03,620 --> 00:03:06,340
data, July 2016.

41
00:03:06,596 --> 00:03:11,620
Nina's own month isn't on the street; its
ten years are still ahead of her.

42
00:03:12,633 --> 00:03:15,673
About two in three of the 1990s houses
stay lit.

43
00:03:15,813 --> 00:03:19,513
But the darkest house on the whole street
is the very first one.

44
00:03:19,753 --> 00:03:23,993
Its renting twin caught the long stock
boom from its first month.

45
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,720
A few years later, the market itself
stalled.

46
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:33,516
For a short run of start months, a dollar
in stocks was worth less than a dollar ten

47
00:03:33,517 --> 00:03:34,519
years on.

48
00:03:34,520 --> 00:03:40,360
Those houses stay lit: the pile lost
ground while the buyer kept paying down

49
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:41,400
her loan.

50
00:03:42,067 --> 00:03:45,667
Then the street goes dark, and stays dark.

51
00:03:46,107 --> 00:03:51,827
For houses bought in the bubble years, the
buyer ended ahead in none of them.

52
00:03:51,971 --> 00:03:56,467
With cash, some of them had stayed lit;
with stocks,

53
00:03:56,507 --> 00:03:57,907
those lights go out too.

54
00:03:58,067 --> 00:04:01,507
These buyers paid near the peak of home
prices,

55
00:04:01,547 --> 00:04:06,067
while their twins kept buying stocks
through the market's fall and rebound.

56
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:11,940
Last time, with the pile in cash, the
darkest house was one bought near the top.

57
00:04:12,052 --> 00:04:17,060
With stocks, that buyer ends more than a
quarter of a million dollars behind.

58
00:04:17,220 --> 00:04:20,100
The dark house got darker.

59
00:04:21,100 --> 00:04:23,980
Dark doors don't mean the renting twins
had an easy ride.

60
00:04:24,140 --> 00:04:28,140
Along the way, many of the stock piles
dipped below where they started.

61
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:34,140
One renting twin's pile sank to $54,310,

62
00:04:34,180 --> 00:04:36,020
and she kept investing.

63
00:04:36,190 --> 00:04:39,660
The replay assumes every renting twin did
exactly that,

64
00:04:39,713 --> 00:04:41,900
every month, through every drop.

65
00:04:42,076 --> 00:04:47,660
Real people sometimes stop, and a renter
who sells in a crash is not on this

66
00:04:47,717 --> 00:04:49,100
street.

67
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:53,140
So, after the whole walk, how many houses
are lit?

68
00:04:54,090 --> 00:04:58,510
Lit: 152 of the 307, about half.

69
00:04:58,683 --> 00:05:02,590
Every house that changed sides went from
the buyer to the renter,

70
00:05:02,643 --> 00:05:03,870
never the other way.

71
00:05:03,998 --> 00:05:06,990
That half is a count of what already
happened,

72
00:05:07,050 --> 00:05:08,910
not a promise about Nina's month.

73
00:05:09,900 --> 00:05:12,140
The brightest house last time still glows.

74
00:05:12,268 --> 00:05:15,740
With stocks, that buyer still ends well
ahead.

75
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:21,420
She bought after prices had fallen, and
her house rose on its whole price,

76
00:05:21,480 --> 00:05:23,740
not just on her $92,000.

77
00:05:24,733 --> 00:05:28,573
In the years after the crash, most of the
lights come back on.

78
00:05:28,653 --> 00:05:32,573
So the half isn't spread evenly: whole
stretches are lit,

79
00:05:32,613 --> 00:05:34,573
and one long stretch is dark.

80
00:05:34,773 --> 00:05:39,293
Those buyers started with lower prices and
low mortgage rates,

81
00:05:39,313 --> 00:05:40,653
and that held the lights on.

82
00:05:40,813 --> 00:05:44,493
Over a shorter stay, with the pile in
stocks,

83
00:05:44,513 --> 00:05:47,133
the buyer was ahead a little more than
half the time.

84
00:05:47,453 --> 00:05:52,733
In this replay, the longer the stay, the
more often the pile finished ahead.

85
00:05:52,925 --> 00:05:57,213
Half a street, lit in stretches; now, a
longer stay.

86
00:05:58,200 --> 00:06:02,840
Stay longer still, and with stocks, most
of the lights go out.

87
00:06:03,016 --> 00:06:10,016
With cash, every one of the 247
fifteen-year starts left the buyer ahead.

88
00:06:10,456 --> 00:06:14,040
With stocks, only about one in five did.

89
00:06:14,220 --> 00:06:18,360
The longer the stay, the longer the pile
has to compound,

90
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:20,360
and the further it pulls ahead.

91
00:06:20,486 --> 00:06:26,120
Longer stays favored the pile; the rent
tag is the one assumption left to test.

92
00:06:27,633 --> 00:06:33,153
So how can stocks outgrow home prices
about 9 in 10 times,

93
00:06:33,173 --> 00:06:36,193
and the buyer still finish ahead in half?

94
00:06:36,383 --> 00:06:42,913
Because the buyer's house grows on all
$400,000 of it,

95
00:06:42,940 --> 00:06:46,593
while the renter's pile starts at $92,000.

96
00:06:46,833 --> 00:06:51,953
The mortgage lets a slower thing grow on a
much bigger base.

97
00:06:52,133 --> 00:06:56,353
The renter catches up only through the
monthly gap,

98
00:06:56,453 --> 00:06:57,873
and through time.

99
00:06:58,866 --> 00:07:03,826
So what if nobody saves that gap, and only
the $92,000 is invested?

100
00:07:04,002 --> 00:07:08,226
Then the buyer is ahead in about 2 in 3
start months.

101
00:07:08,386 --> 00:07:13,587
The monthly habit, not the one big
deposit, did most of the renter's catching

102
00:07:13,693 --> 00:07:14,693
up.

103
00:07:15,066 --> 00:07:19,066
The pile in this replay also pays no fees
and no taxes.

104
00:07:19,258 --> 00:07:24,106
With 1 percent a year taken out, the lit
share rises to 55 percent.

105
00:07:24,282 --> 00:07:28,906
Each real cost on the renter's side moves
houses back toward the buyer.

106
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:31,981
And then there's the tag on Nina's door.

107
00:07:32,114 --> 00:07:35,661
At 5 percent, the street splits almost
exactly in half.

108
00:07:35,821 --> 00:07:41,181
The split sits near 5.1 percent, and
public rent data from the Census Bureau

109
00:07:41,301 --> 00:07:43,341
falls on both sides of it.

110
00:07:43,501 --> 00:07:47,981
So the answer sits on the rent tag, and
the tag is an assumption.

111
00:07:48,966 --> 00:07:52,966
None of this ranks stocks above a house,
or a house above stocks.

112
00:07:53,406 --> 00:07:59,366
A home is also stability and control, and
no street of numbers counts those.

113
00:08:00,366 --> 00:08:02,846
The rest of the limits are on the card: US
only,

114
00:08:02,916 --> 00:08:07,726
history, not a forecast, no taxes on gains
or on the home,

115
00:08:07,766 --> 00:08:10,526
and renters who never skip a month.

116
00:08:11,533 --> 00:08:13,133
So, back to Nina's question.

117
00:08:13,245 --> 00:08:16,813
With the pile in cash, most of her street
was lit;

118
00:08:16,866 --> 00:08:22,013
in stocks, about half; over fifteen years,
most of it dark.

119
00:08:22,386 --> 00:08:27,773
It's the same street as at the start, read
again with the pile in the market.

120
00:08:27,997 --> 00:08:33,293
What no house on this street can show is
how long Nina herself will stay.

121
00:08:33,933 --> 00:08:39,133
Nina's answer: investing the difference
made her street a coin flip,

122
00:08:39,333 --> 00:08:41,933
and her rent tag tips it.

